  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday through Monday)\nIssued at 302 AM CDT Tue Sep 22 2026\n\nThe upper-level ridge will remain positioned from southeast Texas\nto the ArkLaTex and Missouri Valley Thursday and Friday while\nanother shortwave trough lifts from the Baja region/Desert\nSouthwest to the Rocky Mountains. A fetch of deep moisture will\ncontinue to stream from Mexico into southwest and west Texas and\nsoutheast New Mexico in the continued southwesterly flow aloft\nbetween these features. A very good (60-80%) chance of showers and\nthunderstorms will continue from the Guadalupe/Davis Mountains,\nthe Van Horn Corridor and into most of the southeast New Mexico\nPlains in this pattern. Heavy rainfall and flash flooding will\nremain a hazard of concern over these locations through the latter\npart of the week and the Flood Watch may need to be extended with\nlater forecast issuances. Low to medium (20-50%) chances of\nshowers and thunderstorms will continue from the Big Bend to the\nUpper Trans Pecos and western/northern Basin Thursday into \nFriday. The chance of rain will be below mentionable levels from \nthe eastern Basin to the lower Trans Pecos, where subsidence will \nbe stronger along the western influence of the upper ridge. The \nmid level flow pattern will transition zonal by Saturday before \nridging aloft amplifies across our region Sunday. Rain/storm \ncoverage becomes more isolated to scattered in nature this weekend\nwith the best potential favored over the mountains. The next \nupper-level trough translating toward the southern Rockies and \nPlains may bring an increase in rain/storm chances by next Monday \nand Monday night. Temperatures will remain near to just a few \ndegrees above normal in the 80s to lower 90s Thursday and Friday \nbut look to trend warmer this weekend with lower rain chances and \nthe building ridge aloft.",
